== 8.  Brown's criterion: is the tail visible? ==
      Brown (rh42; dictionary/04 Thm 2.1): a non-increasing summable
      a_1 >= a_2 >= ... with A = sum a_n has subset sums exactly [0, A]
      iff a_k <= T_{k+1} = sum_{j>k} a_j for every k.  The subset-sum set
      is permutation invariant, so the test applies to the sorted |Im|
      magnitudes (at a general abscissa sigma).  At a balance point
      Im S = 0 gives the exact identity
          A_pos = |Jm| = (1/2) sum_{n>=2} n^-sigma |sin(t log n)| ,
      so the TOP Brown condition a_1 <= T_2 reads  2 a_1 <= sum n^-sigma|sin|.
  PASS  N=6, sigma=0: the finite prefix violates Brown at the last index (no tail); at sigma=0 even the top index fails because the true tail is infinite, so the criterion cannot be applied -- A = infinity   [fails at k=[1, 2, 3] of m=3]
  PASS  sigma = 0: (1/N) sum |sin(t log n)| stays bounded away from 0 (its limit is the Weyl average 2/pi = 0.636620, approached slowly), so the absolute Im series diverges and there is NO summable tail to see   [ratios 0.5490, 0.7045, 0.6818  (2/pi = 0.636620)]
  PASS  at a balance point A_pos = |Jm| = (1/2) sum |sin| exactly, so the target is A_pos/2 and the top Brown condition is 2 a_1 <= sum n^-sigma |sin|   [A_pos = 1.54236262685  sum|sin|/2 = 1.54236262685]
      For sigma > 1 the series is summable and Brown applies.  Scanning the
      balance root and the top condition (double precision, N = 20000):
  PASS  sigma > 1 (summable): the top Brown condition holds through a threshold and fails above it; above the threshold the largest |Im| term alone exceeds half the positive total, so the tail cannot complete the split   [1.60:ok; 1.70:ok; 1.80:ok; 1.85:ok; 1.90:FAIL; 2.00:FAIL; 2.50:FAIL]
  PASS  the Im-split visibility threshold is near sigma = 1.87, distinct from the pure-budget Brown threshold p* = 1.7286472389981836 (zeta(p*) = 2)   [first failing sigma in the scan: 1.90]

